<Guide lang="en" title="Islam, as a Christian asks about it" description="A guide to Islam for Protestants and Catholics: the same God of Abraham, Jesus and Mary in the Qur'an, and the differences stated honestly.">
  <p>If you go to church, you have probably heard a lot about Islam, and some of it may have been worrying. This page is not here to criticise Christianity. It sets out honestly what Muslims believe, what they share with Christians and where they differ.</p>

  <blockquote><p>"Do not argue with the People of the Book except in the best way. And say: we believe in what was sent down to us and what was sent down to you; our God and your God is one." (Qur'an 29:46)</p></blockquote>

  <h2>What we believe in common</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>One Creator God.</strong> Muslims believe in the God of Abraham, Isaac and Jacob. "Allah" is simply the Arabic word for God, and Arab Christians use it too. <a href="/en/questions/allah/">Who is Allah?</a></li>
    <li><strong>The same prophets.</strong> Adam, Noah, Abraham, Moses, David, Jonah, John the Baptist and Jesus. Many figures from the Bible also appear in the Qur'an.</li>
    <li><strong>Love for Jesus.</strong> Muslims believe Jesus is the Messiah, born of the Virgin Mary, a great prophet who worked miracles. <a href="/en/questions/jesus/">Do Muslims believe in Jesus?</a></li>
    <li><strong>Honour for Mary.</strong> The Qur'an has a whole chapter named after Mary, which Catholic readers in particular may be glad to hear. <a href="/en/questions/mary/">Mary in the Qur'an</a></li>
    <li><strong>Revelation and scripture.</strong> Muslims believe the Law given to Moses and the Gospel given to Jesus were also revelations from God.</li>
    <li><strong>Judgement and eternal life.</strong> Both believe in resurrection and judgement after death, and in heaven and hell.</li>
    <li><strong>Love God and love your neighbour.</strong> Teachings on prayer, fasting, charity, honesty, chastity and honouring parents are strikingly similar.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The differences, honestly</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Topic</th><th>Christianity</th><th>Islam</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>God</td><td>Father, Son and Holy Spirit: the Trinity</td><td>Only one, undivided (Qur'an 112)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Jesus</td><td>Son of God, God incarnate</td><td>A prophet and Messiah sent by God, fully human</td></tr>
        <tr><td>The cross</td><td>Died on the cross and rose again</td><td>Was not killed on the cross; God raised him up (4:157-158)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Sin and salvation</td><td>Original sin; grace through faith in Christ</td><td>No original sin; God's mercy, faith and good deeds. Repentance directly to God, with no intermediary</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Final prophet</td><td>Jesus Christ is God's final Word</td><td>Muhammad is the last prophet and the Qur'an the final revelation</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>These differences are not small, and we know they sit at the centre of a Christian's faith. Muslims would rather treat them as a starting point for understanding each other accurately than as an argument about who is right.</p>

  <h2>Questions we often hear</h2>
  <h3>Do Muslims see Christians as enemies?</h3>
  <p>No. The Qur'an calls Jews and Christians the "People of the Book" (Ahl al-Kitab) and gives them special respect. Islam regards the relationship as close enough that a Muslim man may marry a Christian woman. The Qur'an also says that "the nearest in affection to the believers are those who say: we are Christians" (5:82).</p>
  <h3>Do Muslims believe the Bible was changed?</h3>
  <p>Muslims believe the revelations given to Moses and Jesus were God's word, but that human interpretation and writing were added over a long process of transmission. So they respect the Bible while taking the Qur'an as the final standard.</p>
  <h3>Can I visit a mosque if I go to church?</h3>
  <p>Of course. Most mosques welcome visitors of other faiths. Watch a prayer quietly and ask whatever you want to know. Nobody will push you to convert. <a href="/en/mosques/">Find a mosque near you</a></p>

